-## Key documents
-
-`README.md`, `docs/overview.md`, `docs/vision.md` (now ends with the
-attack-protection design note — the capability model as a behavioural
-baseline), `docs/specifications/00-decisions.md` (15 numbered
-decisions, the reasoning not recoverable from the code),
-`image/README.md`, `image/first-boot/README.md`.
+| `nexus` | this machine in a few lines |
+| `nexus doctor` | health: clock, memory, storage, services, packages |
+| `nexus history` | what changed and when, with system versions |
+| `nexus install <thing>` | plain-words preview: source, size, add-on, restart |
+| `nexus setup <add-ons>` | media, school, development, vpn, security, gaming (KDE only) |
+| `nexus why <package>` | why it is here; image or added; "did you mean" |
+| `nexus gamecheck <game>` | ProtonDB, anti-cheat status, what the launcher can see; reports only |
+| `nexus language` | language vs keyboard; list / add / set |
+| `nexus update` | what is new (system + apps), `--apply` to take it |
+
+## How updates work (decided 4 Oct)
+
+Checked once a day, **installed only when asked**, never a restart by
+itself.
+
+- A timer in each person's session (`nexus-update-notify`) compares the
+  image the machine runs with the registry's (both fingerprints, via
+  skopeo; no password) and sends a notification once per new version.
+- `sudo nexus update --apply` stages the new system version (it takes
+  effect at the next restart; the old one stays in the boot menu) and
+  updates Flatpak apps.
+- Fedora's `bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer` is masked: it restarts by
+  itself, and fails silently on any machine with added packages.
+  rpm-ostree's automatic check is off: on an image-based machine it
+  missed a new Nexus image.
+- The boot menu waits 5 seconds (Fedora: 1), written once to
+  `/boot/grub2/custom.cfg`.
+
