+## Next
+
+1. gamecheck: "what a game here can identify this machine by"
+   (readable: MAC, disk serials, machine-id; root-only: DMI serials,
+   TPM). Reports only; never touches anti-cheat.
+2. **Nexus Shop**: a graphical app on `nexus update` and
+   `nexus install`. Updates as notifications, one button. When
+   something is missing, it searches known sources only (Fedora, RPM
+   Fusion, Flathub with its verified badge; official GitHub releases
+   marked as checked by nobody but the author) — never a random
+   installer. Good on minimal; the full show on KDE; looks that follow
+   the add-on (gaming, development).
+3. Showcase ("insane UI", Linux showing off) and the minimalism look.
+4. Dual boot as a real, explained option.
+5. Later: trim minimal under 600 MB RAM, security review, server and
+   tiling editions, greenboot, NVIDIA (needs NVIDIA hardware).
+
+## Lessons worth keeping
+
+- Test on the real thing. Stand-in tests passed for every one of the
+  bugs the Asus and the VM found this week.
+- In VM instructions, never use key combinations the host computer
+  uses itself (Ctrl+Alt+F3 switched the host, not the VM).
+- Paste `sudo` commands one at a time: the password prompt swallows
+  the lines pasted after it.
+- A clock set in the past makes rpm skip packages signed "after" it;
+  doctor checks the clock.
+- LXQt picks its compositor from the first `session.conf` with a
+  `compositor=` line; labwc takes keyboard layouts from
+  `~/.config/lxqt/labwc/environment`.
+- `podman untag` without a name removes every name, and the next prune
+  deletes the image.
+- Fedora 44 ISOs keep the UEFI boot menu in an appended partition, not
+  in `images/efiboot.img`.
+
+## Rules
+
+- The cosign private key never leaves `~/.config/nexus-signing/`; it is
+  only ever uploaded as a GitHub secret.
+- No personal email or location on the website or in recordings.
+- Nothing is changed without `--apply`; every change is in history.
